<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Pacific Fury #2 - Bombeska on Hokkaido, 1994</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Pacific Fury 2, Bombeska&amp;nbsp;on Hokkaido. 1994&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;13 Feb 1994.&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This is the second scenario of a 16 scenario campaign. Pacific Fury is very closely linked to the Northern Fury campaign. You can explore the situation on the &lt;A href="http://northernfury.bhgdesigns.com/index.php"&gt;webpage&lt;/A&gt; read the Northern Fury background document for further insight. The key nuggets&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;UL&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Cold War continued, no thaw in relations. Germany is unified but somewhat unstable, the Warsaw Pact (WP) still active but somewhat less of a force than it was in the 1980s&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Two days ago, 11 Feb 1994 there was a bloody coup in Warsaw. Hard liners seized power from the struggling reformist Solidarity government. There has been little news but it seems Soviet troops have been called in to assist in stabilizing the country.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Yesterday, 12 Feb 1994, the Winter Olympics in Lillehammer, opened with ominous indications of trouble brewing. Soviet and Eastern Bloc countries were thinly represented at the opening ceremonies and many of their athletes were not present at the start of the actual events this morning. The Soviet Olympic village was empty and initial investigation found that many of the athletes had simply disappeared, while only a few had flown home. Norway ordered the IOC to cancel the games and evacuate the Olympic Village.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Japan, South Korea and Taiwan are moving to a heightened alert status and tensions are generally high. The Soviet Far East Command has increased exercise tempo and scale, nothing overt or threating but noticeable.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Both China and North Korea have been extremely quiet over the past few weeks, oddly ratcheting back the typical rhetoric and down-scaling military exercises.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;It is now late evening (1200Z) on 13 Feb 1994.&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;U&gt;You are the Deputy Commander of the Soviet High Command of the Far East (GKVDV)&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;. You have been given the&amp;nbsp;Job of neutralizing Japanese and American forces on the island of Hokkaido to ensure safe passage of naval and air forces into the Pacific. While your commander is dealing with Moscow you have been given this critical but secondary task. In London they called it 'The Blitz' but&amp;nbsp;in Russia&amp;nbsp;we will call it the 'Bombeska'.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;It won’t be easy, especially when many of your assigned forces have separate chains of command and/or have different objectives.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Designer’s notes&lt;/STRONG&gt;:&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;UL&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;This is a complex scenario. Some of the forces available will not be committed unless you request them (at a price) while others are scheduled to depart or may be re-tasked without notice.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;You should turn 'special messages' ON, there is quite a bit of information fed to you during the scenario, and you will want to see it.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Please respect the initial ready times for aircraft, this is to replicate the transition from routine operations to mobilization and general maintenance.&lt;/LI&gt;
&lt;LI&gt;Points are allotted or lost for destruction of air and sea forces as well as completion of tasked missions.&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
